  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1....A few showers are expected tonight, Thursday, \nwith thunderstorms and a risk of severe weather Friday.\n\nA front will stall near/south of the region Thursday morning as\nhigh pressure over southeast Ontario wedges down the mountains.\nThis will return temperatures back into the mid to upper 60s \nunder cloudy skies and on/off shower chances throughout the \nentirety of the day. The front retreats back to the north before\nwashing out over the northern 2/3rds of the forecast area \nThursday afternoon into Thursday night. This is due in part to \nthe wedging high pressure system over southeast Ontario shifting\ntoward the southern New England coast allowing winds to switch \nback to the southerly direction. The wedge will erode quickly as\na result allowing for a quick spike in temperatures ahead of a \nstronger front Friday morning into Friday afternoon. \n\nGusty showers and a few thunderstorms are possible Friday afternoon \nand evening (Marginal Risk (Level 1 out of 5) mainly east of US-15 \nand toward the I-95 metros) as a true strong (Fall) cold front \ncrosses the region. It will get breezy Friday afternoon as mid-level \nheights quickly fall and the surface pressure gradient tightens in \nresponse to a deepening low tracking to our north. Peak wind gusts \nof 35-45 mph are possible in the mountains, and 30-40 mph elsewhere. \nWind Advisories may be needed for some portion of the Alleghenies \nand Blue Ridge Friday afternoon into Friday night although model \ntrends continue to decrease winds over a large spatial area (i.e \nimmediate surge along the front and disjointed trough compared to \nthe pressure rises on the back end). A squeegee line of gusty \nshowers and some thunderstorms are expected late Friday afternoon \nand evening ahead of the front. If thunderstorms can tap into the \nincreasing winds aloft, there certainly could be a few strong to \nsevere wind gusts.A potent upper trough passes through the region \nFriday night, bringing a reinforcing cold front that pushes out any \nlingering precipitation.\n\nHighs Friday push into the upper 70s and low 80s early in the day \nbefore falling into 60s and 70s (40s and 50s mountains) Friday \nevening. Expect overnight lows in the 40s (30s mountains) Friday \nnight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Temperatures 10 to 15 degrees below average this \nweekend. Dry, but with areas of frost possible and near freezing \ntemperatures, mainly west of I-95, Saturday night and Sunday night.\n\nA strong mid-level disturbance will quickly transverse the region \nSaturday. High pressure will build into the region later Saturday \nthrough Tuesday. Gusty northwest and northerly winds will diminish \nthrough the day Saturday as the last of any precipitation moves out \nand east of southern Maryland. Clearing skies and less wind expected \nthe remainder of the weekend. With such conditions, areas of frost \nhave a better opportunity to form across a large part or the region, \nespecially west of the I-95 corridor. Frost and/or freeze \nheadlines may be needed for Saturday night and early Sunday. As \nmentioned before, the mountains typically have their first \nfreeze October 11, Shenandoah Valley area October 21, and east \nof the Blue Ridge November 1. High pressure will move overhead \nand then east early next week. This will allow for temperatures \nto modify more closer to average with dry conditions continuing.",
